.TH CAT 1 "February 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cat \- concatenate files and print on the standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cat
.RB [ \-B " bsize ]"
.RB [ \-beflnstuv ]
.RB [ \- ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cat
copies each input file to standard output in argument order.
If no file operands are given, input is read from standard input.
The file names
.B -
and
.B --
are treated as aliases for standard input.
.PP
Two execution modes are used:
.TP
Raw mode (default)
Selected when no text-processing flags are enabled. Uses
.BR open (2),
.BR read (2),
and
.BR write (2)
with a high-throughput buffer and full partial-write handling.
.TP
Cooked mode
Selected when any of
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-t ,
or
.BR \-v
is enabled. Performs byte-oriented text transformations (line numbering,
blank squeezing, tab/end markers, and non-printable visualization).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-B " bsize"
Set the raw mode buffer size in bytes. Decimal and hexadecimal forms are accepted.
When omitted,
.B cat
uses stdout block size when it is larger than the built-in fallback.
.TP
.B \-b
Number non-blank output lines. Implies
.BR \-n .
.TP
.B \-e
Equivalent to
.B \-v
plus end markers (show
.B $
before each newline).
.TP
.B \-f
Fast-open behavior for raw mode file opens: open with temporary non-blocking
semantics when possible, then restore blocking mode.
.TP
.B \-l
Acquire and hold a blocking stdout write lock for the full run using
.BR fcntl (2)
record locking.
.TP
.B \-n
Number all output lines.
.TP
.B \-s
Squeeze repeated adjacent empty lines into one empty line.
.TP
.B \-t
Equivalent to
.B \-v
plus tab visualization (
.B ^I
).
.TP
.B \-u
Set stdout to unbuffered stdio mode (
.BR setbuf (3)
with NULL).
.TP
.B \-v
Visualize non-printable bytes using caret and
.B M-
notation.
Bytes with the high bit set are shown as
.B M-
plus the low 7-bit form.
.SH LOCKING
With
.BR \-l ,
.B cat
uses a whole-file write lock on stdout (start 0, length 0,
.BR F_WRLCK ,
.BR F_SETLKW ).
Lock acquisition retries on
.BR EINTR .
.PP
Policy: if stdout locking fails (including unsupported locking backends),
.B cat
exits with non-zero status and prints a diagnostic.
.PP
Potential deadlock: if another process holds an incompatible lock while waiting
for this process to release a resource needed to make progress,
both processes can block indefinitely.
.SH VISUALIZATION RULES
In cooked mode with
.BR \-v :
.IP "1." 4
0x00\-0x1F and 0x7F use caret form (for example,
.B ^@
,
.BR ^? ).
.IP "2." 4
TAB and newline stay literal unless transformed by
.BR \-t
or
.BR \-e .
.IP "3." 4
0x80\-0xFF emit
.B M-
followed by the low 7-bit visualization.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All inputs processed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
One or more failures occurred (open/read/write/lock/close).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cat file1 file2
Concatenate files in order.
.TP
.B cat -n file
Number all lines.
.TP
.B cat -b -s file
Number only non-blank lines and squeeze blank runs.
.TP
.B cat -vte file
Show tabs, non-printables, and line ends.
.TP
.B cat -l output_source > locked_output
Hold an advisory write lock on stdout for the command duration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR tee (1),
.BR fcntl (2),
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2)
